  OUT <div class="row carousel"> <div class="col-md-12 col-12 viewer"> <div class="item"> <div class="videoSection ratio ratio-16x9"> <iframe src="https://www.youtube-nocookie.com/embed/0NESGqweSwI" loading="lazy" title="YouTube video player" frameborder="0" allow="accelerometer; autoplay; clipboard-write; encrypted-media; gyroscope; picture-in-picture; web-share" referrerpolicy="strict-origin-when-cross-origin" allowfullscreen></iframe> </div> </div> <div class="item"> <h4>What is a Narrative?</h4> <div class="carousel-caption"> <p>A narrative is a text that tells a story. It can be fact or fiction. The purpose of a narrative is to entertain the reader or listener. There are different types of narratives – picture books, short stories, novels and traditional tales. Narratives also contain many sub-genres. Some examples include mystery, fantasy, sci-fi, romance and adventure.</p> <p>Narratives follow a clear and predictable structure:</p> <p>BEGINNING: This is called the <i>orientation</i>. Here the reader or listener learns where and when the story is taking place. They will also be introduced to the main characters.</p> <p>MIDDLE: This is called the <i>complication</i>. Here the main character faces a problem or complication and sets out to solve or overcome it. The problem is the force that pushes the action forward. Without a problem, there is no story. As this action happens, it rises to a climax – the most exciting part of the story. This is where the main character’s success
